{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"99382218a7fee26e","name":"card_unit_hummel","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/Allegiance/units/card_unit_hummel","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"HUMMEL","text":"Deployment: If you control a tank, target enemy unit must retreat.","flavorText":"The Hummel (e. Bumblebee), full name “Panzerfeldhaubitze 18M auf Geschützwagen III/IV (Sf) Hummel, Sd.Kfz. 165”,  was a German self propelled gun use by the Wehrmacht during WWII. The Hummel was based on the chassis of Geschützwagen III/IV, manned by a crew of 6 (a driver and 5 man gun crew), and featured a main armament of a 15 cm sFH 18/1 L/30 howitzer gun and a secondary armament of a 7.92 mm Maschinengewehr 34. The Hummel was introduced in 1943 and was first used in large scale combat in the Battle of Kursk where 100 served in the artillery battalions of the Panzer divisions. Because the Hummel could carry a limited amount of ammunition, special Hummel Ammunition Carriers (Munitionsträger Hummel) were developed. Basically this was a Hummel without the 15 cm howitzer, and it could be fitted with the gun when necessary. A total of 714 Hummels were manufactured, plus 150 Hummel ammunition carriers.","faction":"Germany","type":"artillery","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":5,"operationCost":2,"attack":2,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_hummel","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_hummel_title","SourceString":"HUMMEL","LocalizedString":"HUMMEL"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::artillery","attack":2,"defense":4,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_hummel_text","SourceString":"Deployment: If you control a tank, target enemy unit must retreat.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: If you control a tank, target enemy unit must retreat."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_hummel_history","SourceString":"The Hummel (e. Bumblebee), full name “Panzerfeldhaubitze 18M auf Geschützwagen III/IV (Sf) Hummel, Sd.Kfz. 165”,  was a German self propelled gun use by the Wehrmacht during WWII. The Hummel was based on the chassis of Geschützwagen III/IV, manned by a crew of 6 (a driver and 5 man gun crew), and featured a main armament of a 15 cm sFH 18/1 L/30 howitzer gun and a secondary armament of a 7.92 mm Maschinengewehr 34. The Hummel was introduced in 1943 and was first used in large scale combat in the Battle of Kursk where 100 served in the artillery battalions of the Panzer divisions. Because the Hummel could carry a limited amount of ammunition, special Hummel Ammunition Carriers (Munitionsträger Hummel) were developed. Basically this was a Hummel without the 15 cm howitzer, and it could be fitted with the gun when necessary. A total of 714 Hummels were manufactured, plus 150 Hummel ammunition carriers.","LocalizedString":"The Hummel (e. Bumblebee), full name “Panzerfeldhaubitze 18M auf Geschützwagen III/IV (Sf) Hummel, Sd.Kfz. 165”,  was a German self propelled gun use by the Wehrmacht during WWII. The Hummel was based on the chassis of Geschützwagen III/IV, manned by a crew of 6 (a driver and 5 man gun crew), and featured a main armament of a 15 cm sFH 18/1 L/30 howitzer gun and a secondary armament of a 7.92 mm Maschinengewehr 34. The Hummel was introduced in 1943 and was first used in large scale combat in the Battle of Kursk where 100 served in the artillery battalions of the Panzer divisions. Because the Hummel could carry a limited amount of ammunition, special Hummel Ammunition Carriers (Munitionsträger Hummel) were developed. Basically this was a Hummel without the 15 cm howitzer, and it could be fitted with the gun when necessary. A total of 714 Hummels were manufactured, plus 150 Hummel ammunition carriers."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Expansion2019","isReserved":true,"extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Deplo